;; Developer code-tour — source of truth. Generate with `bb devtour`.
;; See docs/devtour/README.md for the format and how to add a block.
;;
;; Each step anchors on {:ns <namespace> :defn <symbol>} — NEVER a line number.
;; Variants: {:file "resources/packages/.../impls.clj" :defn <sym>} for package
;; impls outside src/, and {:defn ig/init-key :dispatch <val>} for a defmethod.
;; The generator bakes the anchored form's real source into index.html; CI
;; (`bb devtour-check`) fails if an anchor stops resolving or the HTML drifts.
{:title "Graphden — developer tour"
 :intro
 "Welcome. This is a guided read of the graphden codebase for someone who
  just joined and needs to find their feet in the whole system — not the
  user tutorial (that lives in `docs/tutorial/` and is about *using* the
  editor), but the host code that makes it run.

  The system splits into a handful of relatively independent **blocks**
  (left). Read them roughly top-to-bottom: each block's `after:` line names
  what it assumes you have already seen. Start with the **Executor** — it is
  the spine everything else hangs off — then follow the dependency order:
  Storage, then Branches, Types, CRUD, Packages, Web, Services, and
  Multi-tenancy.

  Every step shows you the **actual source** of one function, pulled straight
  from the file it lives in. When a step points you at code in another block,
  use the *see also* chips — **Back** always returns you along the path you
  actually walked."

 :blocks
 [{:id :executor
   :title "Executor — how a fn runs"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The spine: turn a graph 'description of a computation' into a value."
   :paths ["src/graphden/executor/"]
   :after []
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.executor.interface :defn execute
     :say "**Start here.** `execute` is the public front door of the whole
           system. A caller hands it an execution *context*, a `fn-id` — the id
           of a row in the graph that *describes* a computation — and a map of
           free args. This ns is a thin, validating façade: it checks the args
           shape and delegates to the real machinery in `compile-runtime`.
           Everything below is how that graph *description* becomes a value."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn execute
     :say "The dispatch heart of the hot path. It checks the cancellation /
           effect / execute-guard gates, pulls the **compiled registry** out of
           the context, looks up the closure for this `fn-id` (or throws
           fn-not-found), translates the caller's name-keyed args into the dual
           slot-id+name shape the compiled code expects, and finally calls
           `(closure translated ctx)`. This is the moment *execute a fn* becomes
           *call a precompiled closure*."
     :see [[:executor "registry"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.context :defn create-context
     :say "Builds the `ExecutionContext` the previous step reads from. It
           carries the storage handle, the `:base-fns` impl registry
           (snapshotted from the global default), a lazily-filled
           `:compiled-registry` holder, the graph/deps caches, and the
           authz/effect seams. Because scoping (e.g. which branch) lives here,
           the *same* `fn-id` run in two contexts can resolve to different
           code — this is where that happens."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn registry
     :say "Returns the `{fn-id -> closure}` map from the context holder. On a
           cold holder it rebuilds on demand under an invalidation lock, with a
           double-checked read so concurrent callers coalesce onto one rebuild
           instead of stampeding. This is the lazy **compile-once, reuse-forever**
           registry the executor actually runs against."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn rebuild!
     :say "The compile-at-startup pipeline in one form: `read-graph` from
           storage, then `build-lookups`, then `compile-all`; finally it primes
           the graph/deps caches and resets the registry holder. This is the
           seam where **code = graph in DB** turns into runnable Clojure
           closures. The next few steps open up each stage it calls."
     :see [[:executor "build-lookups"] [:executor "compile-all"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.composition.core :defn sync-fns-to-storage!
     :say "The opposite direction — and the reason there is a graph to read at
           all. Authored fn-defs (from packages) are reconciled into fn / slot /
           binding rows in storage here. So the graph that `rebuild!` reads was
           itself *written* by this composition layer from source fn-defs. Read
           it to see how declarations become persisted graph entities."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.lookups :defn build-lookups
     :say "Indexes the raw graph rows — fns, slots, fn-slots, bindings,
           list-items — into the O(1) maps every compile helper consumes
           (`:fn-map`, `:slot-map`, `:bindings-by-fn`, the inheritance-chain
           cache, …). This is the in-memory materialization of the DB graph that
           the whole compile pass walks over."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.lookups :defn root-fn
     :say "Walks a composed fn's `parent-ids` inheritance chain up to the
           parentless **root** that owns the slots and carries a registered
           impl. This is the base-fn resolution step: how an arbitrary composed
           `fn-id` maps to the primitive whose Clojure impl will actually run."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.bindings :defn collect-bindings
     :say "Classifies each of a fn's root slots (via `classify-slot`) into a
           `:value` / `:free` / `:ref` / `:seq` / `:secret-value` entry,
           memoised on the lookups. This classification is the pivot the code
           generator switches on next — the *shape* of every argument is decided
           here, once, at compile time rather than per execution."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn compile-all
     :say "Topologically orders every impl-backed fn and compiles each in
           dependency order into `{fn-id -> (fn [fa ctx])}`, cached process-wide
           by (graph-shape × base-fn set). Dependency order matters: by the time
           a parent compiles, its children's callables already exist to be
           wired in."
     :see [[:executor "compile-fn"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn compile-fn
     :say "Produces one fn's runtime closure `(fn [fa ctx])`. It turns each
           classified binding into a *builder*, wires env-bindings and
           rename-aliases, and at call time assembles the args map and invokes
           `(impl args ctx)`. This is the concrete anatomy of a single compiled
           graph node."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"] [:executor "resolve-impl"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn arg-builder
     :say "Emits the per-binding `(fn [fa ctx])`: `:value` becomes a constant,
           `:free` reads the slot-id (with a name fallback), `:ref` becomes a
           `thunk`/delay that **defers** the child call, `:seq` becomes a lazy
           seq. This is the origin of graphden's laziness — refs are *wrapped,
           not evaluated*, so native Clojure `if`/`and`/`or` short-circuit for
           free without the executor knowing their names."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-arg"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn resolve-impl
     :say "Looks the base-fn's Clojure impl out of `lookups[:base-fns]` by the
           root fn's name, throwing `:compile/missing-impl` on a gap. This is the
           concrete join between a graph node and the actual code that computes
           it — the point where the graph description meets a real function."
     :see [[:executor "defbase"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.defbase :defn defbase
     :say "The macro base-fn authors use. It rewrites a body that names its args
           as bare symbols into `(defn name [__args ctx] …)`, where each symbol
           reads through `resolve-arg`. This explains why the impls that
           `resolve-impl` finds look like ordinary Clojure even though their
           arguments arrive as a compiled map."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-arg"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.runtime :defn resolve-arg
     :say "The read-side of laziness. Inside an impl this forces a `thunk`
           (calling it once), derefs an `IDeref`/`delay`, or returns a plain
           value untouched. This is where a deferred `:ref` argument actually
           gets evaluated — at the moment, and only if, the impl reads it. Pair
           it with `arg-builder`, which created the thunk."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn call-with-cache
     :say "Closes the loop. Child-callable invocations are memoised for the
           duration of one top-level `execute`, keyed by `[ref-id
           projected-free-args]` in a per-call map (with a `::nil` sentinel and a
           size cap). This is how the lazy graph avoids recomputing the same
           sub-result and delivers a single cached value back up to the caller —
           the DRY principle, enforced at runtime."}]}

  {:id :storage
   :title "Storage & the graph"
   :status :toured
   :summary "How the graph is stored and traversed — Postgres, recursive CTEs,
             the StorageCRUD / ExecutionGraph protocols."
   :paths ["src/graphden/storage/" "src/graphden/schema/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn Storage
     :say "The storage layer starts with three protocols; this is the first.
           `Storage` is the lifecycle contract (`initialize` / `close`) every
           backend implements, binding a concrete store to a `DataSchema`
           before any graph can be read or written."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn StorageCRUD
     :say "The row-level contract: `read-entity`, `query-entities`,
           `create-entity`, and friends. Every graph read the executor does
           bottoms out in these calls — learn this shape and you know how the
           whole system touches the database."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn ExecutionGraph
     :say "One method: `resolve-execution-graph` — given a root fn-id, return
           its entire reachable subgraph in a single shot. This is the seam the
           executor's `rebuild!` reads through, and it is implemented twice
           below: a backend-agnostic version and a Postgres-optimized one."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.schema.protocol.protocol :defn DataSchema
     :say "What a schema exposes: the entities, their fields, and the field
           types (`:ref`, `:enum`, `:union`). Rows are shaped by this vocabulary
           before storage ever sees them — so read it before the row-storage
           code below."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.graph.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Where the graph model is actually declared: the `ns` / `fn` / `slot`
           / `binding` / `binding-list-item` entities and the `:ref` edges
           between them. This is the concrete table + foreign-key layout that
           every traversal walks."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.malli.schema :defn make-entity-schema
     :say "Turns a field map into a closed malli `:map` (nullable field →
           optional key). The concrete link between a schema's field-spec and
           the validated shape of a stored row."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn ExecutionGraphResult
     :say "The record every resolver returns: the entity collections plus
           by-fn / by-binding indexes. Know this shape first and the two
           resolvers below read easily — they all just fill it in."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn ->execution-graph
     :say "Builds that record and its O(1) convenience indexes — the shared
           `assemble the result` step both backends funnel into."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn process-fn-node
     :say "Expands one fn during traversal, extracting its outgoing edges:
           parent-ids, base / element / return-type, and binding + list-item
           `ref-fn-id`s. This function IS the authoritative definition of what
           counts as a graph edge."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn resolve-execution-graph-bfs
     :say "The backend-agnostic BFS driver: keep calling `process-fn-node`
           until the reachable closure is exhausted. This is the reference
           algorithm the Postgres recursive CTE mirrors."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.generic-graph :defn resolve-execution-graph
     :say "The default `ExecutionGraph` implementation — it wires the
           `StorageCRUD` loaders into the BFS. Correct on any backend, but it
           costs ~4N round-trips, which is exactly what motivates the Postgres
           specialization next."
     :see [[:storage "StorageCRUD"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.core :defn PostgresStorage
     :say "The concrete Postgres backend. Its `ExecutionGraph` extension
           delegates to the Postgres resolver instead of the generic BFS —
           showing how a backend overrides the default path when it can do
           better."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph :defn reachable-fns-sql
     :say "The `WITH RECURSIVE` CTE that walks every outgoing edge from the
           seed fn in one statement and returns the reachable `fn.id` set. This
           is the heart of the recursive-CTE traversal, mirroring in SQL exactly
           the edge list that `process-fn-node` computes in Clojure."
     :see [[:storage "process-fn-node"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph :defn resolve-execution-graph
     :say "The optimized resolver: run the CTE to get reachable ids, enforce
           the graph-size cap, bulk-load fn / slot / binding rows with five
           `IN`-clause queries, and hand off to `->execution-graph`. O(1)
           round-trips versus the generic O(N) — the payoff of the whole
           protocol split."
     :see [[:storage "->execution-graph"]]}]}

  {:id :versioning
   :title "Branches & versioning"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The VersionedStorage decorator, per-branch resolution, diff/merge
             and branch-local? propagation."
   :paths ["src/graphden/versioning/" "src/graphden/system/branch_router.clj"]
   :after [:storage]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn wrap-with-versioning
     :say "The entry point: take any base storage, ensure a `main` branch
           exists, and return a `VersionedStorage` bound to it. Start here to
           see how the entire versioning layer is bolted onto the plain storage
           from the previous block."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn VersionedStorage
     :say "The decorator record itself. It implements every storage protocol,
           delegating non-versioned entities straight to the base storage and
           intercepting versioned ones — reads resolve to a branch view, writes
           append version rows. Grasp this structure before anything else here."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn entity-config
     :say "The authoritative map of WHICH entities are versioned (fn / fn-slot
           / binding / binding-list-item) and how each splits across two tables:
           `version-data-fields` live in the version table, identity fields stay
           on the base row. Everything downstream keys off this config."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn resolve-version
     :say "The public per-branch read: for one entity, load its version chain,
           merge in a single batched pass, and return the winning version (nil
           on a tombstone). This is how a plain read becomes branch-aware."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn collect-branch-chain
     :say "The branch-chain walk: `[branch → parent → … → root]` via
           `:base-branch-id`, memoised process-wide. This inheritance backbone
           is what every resolve and cache-invalidation consults to know which
           branches a read can see."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn resolve-version-from-cache
     :say "The in-memory chain walk the resolver actually runs: at each branch
           level combine own-latest with merge candidates, pick the greatest
           `effective-ts`, and recurse to the parent. One tested algorithm shared
           by both single-entity and whole-graph reads."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.branch-local :defn effective-branch-local?
     :say "Monotonic-OR over the `:parent-ids` closure: a fn (or ANY ancestor)
           marked `:branch-local? true` is local forever. The resolver's merge
           filter calls this so runtime-config fns — web-server port, vault
           path, cron schedule — never leak across branches on merge."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn check-list-item-position-collisions!
     :say "The list-item position-collision guard. A cross-branch base index on
           `(binding-id, position)` would wrongly block divergence, so per-branch
           uniqueness is enforced here in the resolved view instead — a subtle
           invariant the write path defends with an advisory lock. Fn names
           follow the same doctrine: `check-fn-name-collision!` just below
           replaced the raw `UNIQUE (namespace_id, name)`, whose soft-deleted
           identities blocked their names forever."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn diff-branches
     :say "The branch-symmetric resolved-view diff: gather every entity touched
           on either side's visibility set, resolve both, and report
           added/modified. The simplest read-only comparison — it precedes the
           conflict-oriented framing below."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn detect-conflicts
     :say "Finds entities modified on BOTH branches after the computed fork
           point (skipping branch-local fns). This introduces fork-point
           reasoning and defines exactly what a merge must resolve before it can
           proceed."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn merge-branch!
     :say "The merge itself: assert conflicts are resolved, then atomically
           write ONE `branch-merge` record — which makes the source's versions
           visible through resolution, copying no rows — plus any resolution
           overrides. The payoff step tying resolution and conflict detection
           together."}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router :defn build-branch-ctx
     :say "Builds a fresh live `ExecutionContext` whose storage is a
           `VersionedStorage` rewrapped at the requested branch, with its own
           compiled-registry and graph-cache atoms. This is how a branch gets
           its own executor without re-sharding the graph."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router :defn dispatch
     :say "The top-level Ring middleware: extract the branch ref (header or
           query), resolve it to a branch-id, and route the request to that
           branch's lazily-built handler (400 on an unknown branch). Where a
           live request finally lands in the correct per-branch context."}]}

  {:id :types
   :title "Type system"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Subtyping, unification, narrowing, refinements, the sweep-at-zero
             checker, and the :secret information-flow marker."
   :paths ["src/graphden/types/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn primitives
     :say "Start with the leaf vocabulary. This is the flat enum of primitive
           value-kinds plus the special `:any` (top) and `:never` (bottom). Every
           type in the system is built from these, so nothing structural makes
           sense until you have them."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn child-types
     :say "The type grammar in one function: every compound arm — record maps,
           `[:list]`, `[:map]`, `[:tuple]`, `[:refine]`, `[:secret]`, `[:union]`,
           `[:fn]` — and how each recurses. This doubles as the map of how
           records / unions / variants / fn-types are represented."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn well-formed?
     :say "The syntactic validity guard at the boundary between user input
           (fns.edn / the API) and the rest of the module. Reading it cements the
           exact shape of each type kind: fn args-map, effects set, refine base,
           secret inner."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn make-union
     :say "The smart constructor for `[:union …]`: flatten, dedupe, absorb
           `:never`, and drop any member subsumed by another. This canonical
           union shape is what the rest of the system relies on — and your
           introduction to sum types."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn desugar-variant
     :say "Rewrites `[:variant tag T …]` into a structural union of tag-pinned
           records. Shows how the higher-level variant sugar reduces to the union
           + refinement machinery you just met — no new runtime concept needed."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn subtype?
     :say "The heart of assignability: reflexive / transitive subtyping across
           primitives, `:jsonb` sinks, records, lists, maps, tuples, refinements,
           unions, secrets, and fn-types. Every checker decision — is `actual`
           a subtype of `expected`? — bottoms out here."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn constraint-implies?
     :say "Decides when one refinement constraint entails another (numeric
           bounds, `:in` / `:=` / `:not=`, `:and` / `:or`). This is exactly how
           `subtype?` compares refinement types like `:positive-int` ⊆
           `:non-negative-int`, so read it right after."
     :see [[:types "subtype?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn unify
     :say "Robinson unification with occurs-check and subtype-aware leniency,
           plus a union fallback. This is how polymorphic type variables get
           bound during checking — the two-way complement to the one-way
           `subtype?` test."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn strip-null
     :say "The single source of truth for nullability narrowing: remove `:null`
           from a top-level union (bare `:null` → `:never`) while NEVER stripping
           a `:secret`. The canonical example of type narrowing, shared by
           control-flow refinement and the `:coalesce` rule."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn taint-with-secret-if-tainted
     :say "The `:return-type-rule` propagator that wraps a fn's return in
           `[:secret …]` when any argument carries a secret anywhere. This is how
           the information-flow marker flows through composition — the payoff of
           the secret arm you saw in `subtype?`."
     :see [[:types "subtype?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.types.check.literals :defn classify-literal
     :say "Infers a structural type from a literal Clojure value (records,
           header maps, `[:list T]`, primitives). This is where concrete argument
           values enter the checker as types — the input side of every binding
           check."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-binding!
     :say "Verifies one binding's `actual` ⊆ `expected`, running `unify` when
           type-vars are present and handling the `:any` / `:never` /
           refinement-on-literal escape hatches. The atomic unit of
           type-checking, tying together `subtype?`, `unify`, and
           `classify-literal`."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-fn-def!
     :say "Type-checks a single fn-def against its (single or multiple-
           inheritance) parents: resolve parent slots, run the monotonicity /
           unknown-slot / effect guards, unify bindings, compute the return via
           type-rules, propagate effects, and snapshot the rich-type into the
           registry. The per-node checker the sweep repeats."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-all-defs!
     :say "The whole-graph sweep entry point: fold `check-fn-def!` over
           topologically-ordered fn-defs so each def's computed rich-type is
           registered before its dependents read it. This is how the entire graph
           is checked end to end."}]}

  {:id :crud
   :title "CRUD & the write path"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Entity/branch/secret writes, request parsing, type-check on write,
             fn-execution lookup + persist."
   :paths ["src/graphden/crud/"]
   :after [:storage :types]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.crud.request :defn extract-entity-params
     :say "The HTTP boundary where a mutation first becomes structured data:
           pull `type` / `id` out of a write request, preferring reitit
           `:path-params` and falling back to raw-URI parsing."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.request :defn read-json-body
     :say "Normalizes the Ring body (string / InputStream / already-parsed) into
           a keyword-keyed Clojure map — the payload every entity write is built
           from."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.branches :defn resolve-branch-ref
     :say "Resolves the target branch (by UUID or name) so a write lands on the
           right versioned storage. The branch dimension enters here, before any
           row is touched."
     :see [[:versioning "wrap-with-versioning"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn create-entity
     :say "The generic single-row write entry point, and the best first stop for
           the whole write path: it sequences every stage — capability gate,
           `write-rej` validation, `sp/create-entity`, then invalidate + notify."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.validation :defn write-rej
     :say "The pre-write rejection gate that `create-entity` / `update-entity`
           consult. It centralizes the cycle / MI-collision / terminal /
           list-closed / secret-path checks that can veto a mutation before it
           ever persists."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn apply-create-record-type-body
     :say "A multi-row atomic write unit: fn-row + N slot-rows + N fn-slot
           junctions, appending each success to a shared `journal` atom. Shows
           how one logical create fans out into many graph rows."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn apply-create-rollback
     :say "The `:try` on-throw partner: replay the `journal` in reverse,
           best-effort deleting each row. This is how atomicity is achieved here
           WITHOUT a DB transaction — the compensating half of the body above."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn apply-create-core
     :say "The atomic core for generic creates: create-or-humanise-error, a
           post-create whole-fn type-check, conditional rollback, and the
           rename-slot side-effect. The invariant-bearing heart of the apply
           flow."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.type-check :defn type-check-fn-after-mutation!
     :say "Reconstructs the touched fn-def and runs `check-fn-def!` after a
           mutation, returning a `{:reason …}` that drives reject + rollback.
           This is the type-check-on-write that `apply-create-core` relies on —
           the graph is never allowed to persist ill-typed."
     :see [[:types "check-fn-def!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn notify-after-write!
     :say "The post-persist step: invalidate caches and broadcast a NOTIFY delta
           so sibling pods rebuild the affected fns. How a committed write becomes
           visible fleet-wide."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.lookup :defn resolve-fn
     :say "Translates the `/api/execute` `{:fn-id | :fn-name}` request into the
           full `:fn` row in one round-trip — the lookup that begins the
           execution + persist path."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution :defn apply-execute
     :say "The `/api/execute` orchestrator: resolve fn + version + free-arg
           slots, acquire a concurrency slot, submit the future, and dispatch on
           succeeded / pending / failed. The spine of the execute write path —
           and where it hands off to the executor from the first block."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist :defn create-pending-with-args!
     :say "Atomically writes the `:fn-execution` row plus its per-arg
           (`:fn-execution-arg` / `-item`) rows — the concrete `an execution
           becomes persisted graph rows` step."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist :defn write-finished!
     :say "Updates the execution row with the future's terminal outcome (result
           / error / cancelled, with taint redaction). Closes the loop so a
           polled execution reflects final persisted state."}]}

  {:id :packages
   :title "Packages — the graph's content"
   :status :toured
   :summary "How fns.edn + impls.clj become base-fns and fn-defs; the loader,
             load order, and the first-party package tree."
   :paths ["src/graphden/packages/" "resources/packages/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-default-packages
     :say "The newcomer's entry point: it just calls `load-packages [core web
           app]`, naming the default package set and showing where the whole
           disk-to-graph pipeline is kicked off at boot."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-packages
     :say "The top-level orchestrator: resolve dependency order, load each
           package, and fold every module's results into the combined
           `{:base-fn-defs … :fn-defs … :namespaces …}` map that seeds the graph.
           The hub the rest of this block drills into."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn resolve-dependencies
     :say "A DFS topological sort over each `package.edn`'s `:dependencies`,
           pulling transitive deps so primitives load before their consumers.
           This is the dependency-driven load order."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn validate-dep-constraints!
     :say "Runs after ordering to check declared semver constraints against the
           classpath, failing fast at boot with `:packages/version-conflict`
           instead of a cryptic `Unknown parent` later. The version machinery
           guarding load order."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-single-package
     :say "For one package: read its `package.edn` metadata and reduce over its
           `:modules`, merging each module's base-fn-defs and fn-defs. The bridge
           from `a package` down to `its modules`."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn process-module
     :say "The heart of one module: load its `fns.edn` and `impls.clj`, then
           `group-by` splits the entries into base-fns (which need impls) versus
           fn-defs (compositions / type-rows). Where fns.edn and impls.clj first
           meet."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn base-fn?
     :say "The classifier `process-module` groups on: an entry is a base-fn only
           if it has no `:parent` / `:parents` and is not a type-row. This is the
           fundamental base-fn versus fn-def distinction a newcomer must
           internalize."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-module-fns
     :say "Reads a module's `fns.edn` (pure EDN data) via a source-position
           reader, attaching `:source-file` / `:source-line` to every fn-def so
           later type errors point at `file:line`. How the on-disk declarations
           enter memory."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-impls-via-eval
     :say "The counterpart to fns.edn: slurp `impls.clj`, `eval` each form in a
           fresh namespace, and return that namespace's `impls` var — the
           `{fn-name → impl-fn}` map. How Clojure code on disk becomes live
           functions."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn fn-def->base-fn-def
     :say "The glue: marry an fns.edn entry (args, return-type, effects, tags)
           with its impl-map value into the registry-shaped
           `{:args … :return-type … :impl fn …}`. Literally where fns.edn and
           impls.clj fuse into a base-fn."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-impl"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/core/arithmetic/impls.clj" :defn add
     :say "A concrete `defbase` base-fn impl — `(defbase add [nums] (safe-arith
           + nums :add))`. This is the value `load-impls-via-eval` collects and
           `fn-def->base-fn-def` pairs with the `:add` entry in fns.edn. Grounds
           the abstract loader in a real arithmetic primitive."
     :see [[:executor "defbase"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn sync-namespaces!
     :say "Closes the loop into storage: take the collected namespace paths,
           build the `core` → `core.arithmetic` parent-child `:ns` entity
           hierarchy, and return `{ns-path → id}` that the fn-def sync hangs the
           live fn rows under."
     :see [[:executor "sync-fns-to-storage!"]]}]}

  {:id :web
   :title "Web & the editor"
   :status :toured
   :summary "HTTP routing, the graph-native page/partial runtime, and the
             editor frontend."
   :paths ["resources/packages/web/" "resources/packages/app/editor/"]
   :after [:packages]
   :steps
   [{:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn http-server
     :say "The http-kit wrapper base-fn: `run-server` spins the listener thread
           and binds the composed Ring handler to a port, recording `:network` /
           `:process` effects. Where a request first enters the web layer, so the
           natural first stop. Note it is a tiny primitive — everything else is
           composed from small base-fns in `web/http/fns.edn`."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn realize-request-body
     :say "Fully realizes the incoming request body so downstream graph handlers
           see plain data instead of a one-shot stream — the very next thing that
           happens to a raw request after it lands."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-handler-fn
     :say "Composes the compiled reitit router with the default handler into the
           single Ring callable http-kit actually invokes per request. The seam
           between the server socket and routing."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-router-fn
     :say "Builds the bare `reitit.ring/router` from route data — which is itself
           graph-composed vectors and keywords. This is the routing table the
           handler consults to match a path to a handler."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-create-default-handler-fn
     :say "Constructs the fallback handler reitit uses when nothing matches,
           wrapping the graph-composed 404 / 405 / 406 response maps. The `no
           route` branch."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn middleware
     :say "The middleware factory: turn a graph `body` (with `:request` and
           `:next-handler` free args) into a reitit `{:name :wrap}` spec folded
           around each route handler. How cross-cutting logic — like the
           branch-routing wrap next — is expressed as a graph fn."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :defn current-branch-router
     :say "Reads the currently-installed per-branch router from a global atom
           (nil before boot installs it). The branch-routing wrap needs this
           router first."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :defn dispatch-to-branch
     :say "The branch-routing wrap: delegate the Ring request to the correct
           per-branch handler resolved from the router above. This is how ONE
           server multiplexes requests across branch-scoped graphs — the package
           side of the versioning block's `dispatch`."
     :see [[:versioning "dispatch"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/ring-adapter/impls.clj" :defn authenticate-request
     :say "The auth gate applied to a request inside the handler chain before a
           page or action runs. Sits between routing/dispatch and the handler
           body that will build a response."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn hiccup-element
     :say "The generic `[tag attrs children]` constructor behind the graph-native
           page runtime. User-composed pages are fn-defs (`:html-page`,
           components) that emit hiccup through this primitive — including the
           `data-action` attrs that the editor's JavaScript runtime
           (`resources/packages/app/editor/*.js`) binds on click. Where a user's
           page becomes a concrete hiccup tree."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn h-raw
     :say "Wraps a string in a hiccup `RawString` so inline script / style bodies
           (like the editor's bundled JS / CSS) render verbatim instead of being
           HTML-escaped. The escape hatch that lets a composed page embed the JS
           frontend without mangling it."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn render-hiccup
     :say "Serializes the assembled hiccup tree into an HTML string, with a
           taint-propagation rule so rendered secrets stay redacted. The point
           where a graph-composed page turns into the response body."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn process-response
     :say "The outbound finalizer: stringify headers, force connection close, and
           gzip / brotli-encode the body based on the request's Accept-Encoding.
           The last stop as the rendered page leaves the web layer back through
           http-kit."}]}

  {:id :services
   :title "Services & the fleet"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Desired-state service registry, the reconciler/supervisor, and the
             dynamic multi-executor fleet controller."
   :paths ["src/graphden/services/" "src/graphden/fleet/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.schema.services.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Start with what a service ROW is: this registers the `:service`
           entity plus the `:restart-policy` / `:cardinality` enums. A service is
           a desired-state `keep this fn-id running` declaration — not versioned,
           no args table — the thing everything downstream reconciles toward."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.services.schema :defn effective-pool-size
     :say "Resolves a row's cardinality into an advisory-lock slot count:
           `:per-pod` → no lock, `:singleton` → 1, `:pool` → N. You need this
           singleton-vs-per-pod-vs-pool model before the reconciler's lock gating
           makes sense."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn diff-desired
     :say "The pure policy core: given the enabled DB rows (desired) and the
           in-process `running` set (actual), return `{:to-start :to-stop}`. It
           isolates the reconcile DECISION from side effects — the cleanest first
           look at the loop."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn reconcile-once!
     :say "One reconcile pass: read enabled rows, apply `diff-desired`, detect
           config drift, and race advisory-lock slots. This is BOTH the
           edge-triggered handler (fired by `:service` CRUD / NOTIFY) and the
           level-triggered periodic tick — the placeholder it recomputes each
           pass is exactly what heals a crashed lock-owner on the next tick."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn start-service!
     :say "The supervisor that actually keeps a fn running: invoke the target fn
           via the executor and, on a startup failure, sleep + retry up to N
           times per the row's `:restart-policy`, stamping `:start-failed-at` when
           exhausted so reconcile will not busy-loop. The `how a service stays up`
           answer that closes out the services half."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.placement :defn placement-for
     :say "Opens the fleet half with its shared source of truth: the
           `:placement` row mapping `(org, entry-fn-id)` → the executor holding
           that cell, highest-epoch wins. Every fleet component reads or writes
           this map, so it is the right foundation before routing and packing."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.router :defn forward-or-nil
     :say "The internal forward-hop: when a request lands on a pod that does not
           hold the target cell, look up the holder in the placement map and
           HTTP-forward there instead of returning `421`. How the placement map
           becomes live cross-fleet routing."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.metrics :defn cell-weight
     :say "Folds a cell's structural cost (forward-closure fn-count) and live
           load (the org's in-flight executions) into the single scalar the
           packer and rebalancer order by. The load signal that drives every
           placement decision below."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.packer :defn pack
     :say "The LPT (longest-processing-time-first) greedy packer: sort cells
           heaviest-first and drop each on the least-loaded executor — the classic
           makespan 2-approximation. It answers `what SHOULD the placement be` for
           a from-scratch layout."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.rebalance :defn rebalance
     :say "The churn-minimising planner: rather than diff against a fresh pack,
           do steepest-descent (move one cell busiest → lightest while it strictly
           reduces spread) with a `:min-improvement` hysteresis floor, emitting
           the FEWEST moves. How a running fleet is nudged toward balance without
           thrashing."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.control-loop :defn plan-tick
     :say "The pure brain of one control pass: always emit initial placements for
           unheld cells, but gate rebalance moves behind `:sustain-ticks` so
           imbalance must PERSIST (not just spike) before cells move. Composes the
           packer + rebalancer into the actual control policy."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.control-loop :defn run-tick!
     :say "The side-effecting driver the leader-locked component runs each tick:
           read live cells + current placement, call `plan-tick`, then realise
           each initial placement and sustained move through the `move-fn` seam.
           The loop that ties the whole fleet together."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.controller :defn move-cell!
     :say "Executes a single relocation as `run-tick!`'s `move-fn`: the load →
           flip-epoch → evict dance that keeps the cell continuously served
           (target compiles first, placement epoch bumps to reroute, source evicts
           last). How an abstract `move` decision safely becomes a live cell
           migration across pods."}]}

  {:id :tenancy
   :title "Multi-tenancy"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Orgs, RLS, grants, the two-layer effect gate, and per-request
             tenant routing."
   :paths ["src/graphden/tenancy/" "src/graphden/auth/"]
   :after [:crud]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.tenancy.app-router :defn make-app-router
     :say "The opening stop — literally where a tenant request arrives. This
           builds the `:app-router` seam the branch-router consults first:
           resolve the tenant org from the request Host, look up that org's
           handler fn, and run it org-scoped, sandboxed, and time-bounded."}
    {:ns graphden.auth.provider :defn AuthProvider
     :say "The pluggable identification protocol: Ring request → principal map
           (`:authenticated?`, plus `:user` / `:org`). This injectable seam is how
           core's single-token check is swapped for a tenant-aware one."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.auth :defn token-map-provider
     :say "The addon's `AuthProvider` that resolves a bearer token to
           `{:user :org}` (hash-matched). The piece that adds the `:org`, turning
           the addon from a safe no-op into real multi-tenancy."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.context :defn org-from-principal
     :say "Extracts the org id from the authenticated principal and feeds the
           `*current-org*` thread-local that all scoping reads. Exactly how `who
           you are` becomes `which org's data you see`."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.addon :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :tenancy/request-scope
     :say "The central orchestrator wiring every other piece together:
           authenticate, bind the org, reject cross-org / wrong-pod, gate effects,
           enforce grants, then run the handler. The heart of the tour — read the
           steps below as expansions of what this one calls."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.storage :defn OrgScopedStorage
     :say "Isolation layer one: a storage decorator that stamps `:org-id` on
           writes and filters reads to `{current-org, public}`. The concrete
           answer to `how is one org's data kept apart`, once the org is bound."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.rls :defn enable-rls!
     :say "Isolation layer two: install own+public-read / own-write Postgres RLS
           policies on every org-scoped table at boot. Belt-and-suspenders — a raw
           SQL path cannot bypass it the way it could bypass the decorator above."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.rls :defn org-aware-datasource
     :say "Wraps the pool so every borrowed connection sets the
           `graphden.current_org` GUC from `*current-org*`, making RLS enforce per
           request. The runtime half of RLS, and why the session variable is
           reset on each borrow."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.grant :defn can?
     :say "The pure grant decision: does a subject hold a capability in a
           namespace (subject × capability × namespace, with `:admin` / `:write`
           subsumption and dot-path coverage). The authorization primitive every
           gate below calls into."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.authz :defn authorize-writer
     :say "Builds the per-namespace write guard `OrgScopedStorage` runs: resolve
           the target `:namespace-id` and deny (403) a write the principal lacks
           the capability for. Where coarse org-scoping sharpens into per-namespace
           write enforcement."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.authz :defn authorize-executor
     :say "Builds the `:execute-guard` seam the executor consults once per
           top-level call, throwing `:authz/forbidden` when the principal lacks
           `:execute` on the fn's namespace. The admission seam for RUNNING a fn —
           the execute-side mirror of the write guard."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn cloud-request-allowed-effects
     :say "Effect gate, layer one: the `*allowed-effects*` a tenant HTTP request
           runs under — blocks env / io / network / process, but keeps `:raw-sql`
           so the trusted handler can read storage on the tenant's behalf. Bound
           by `request-scope`."
     :see [[:tenancy "request-scope"]]}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn default-cloud-allowed-effects
     :say "Effect gate, layer two: the stricter set (`#{:db :state :time
           :random}`, no `:raw-sql`) placed on the exec ctx when a tenant's OWN
           submitted graph runs. Closes the tour — even code a tenant authors
           cannot reach the raw-SQL escape hatch or the outside world."}]}]}
